---
import Flower from "../../components/Flower.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const lang = "en";

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/",
        name: "Lilo Browser",
        position: 1,
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/ai-agents/",
        name: "Browser for AI agents",
        position: 2,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/ai-agents/#faq",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    inLanguage: "en",
    mainEntity: [],
    url: "https://getlilo.app/ai-agents/",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  lang={lang}
  title="Lilo Browser: a real Mac browser for AI agents, with a built-in MCP server"
  description="Give Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or any MCP client a real WebKit browser on your Mac. Agents work in their own Space, and Lilo asks before they send, pay or delete. Free and open source."
  schema={schema}
>
  <main id="main-content">
    <div class="top">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <Flower id="hero-flower" class="mark bloom" />
          <h1>
            A browser for
            <br />
            <span>your AI agents.</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="sub">
            Lilo has a built-in MCP server. Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or any
            MCP client open tabs, click, type, and read pages, the console and
            the network, in a real browser beside you, in their own Space. There
            is no AI inside Lilo: bring the agent you already use.
          </p>
          <div class="cta">
            <a
              class="pill dark"
              href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/releases/latest/download/Lilo.dmg"
            >
              Download for Mac
            </a>
            <a class="pill soft" href="#connect">
              Connect an agent
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="band" id="tools">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <span class="tag">What agents can do</span>
          <h2>A real browser, not a headless one.</h2>
          <p>
            Agents drive the same WebKit tabs you use, so you can watch them
            work and take over at any moment.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-list two">
          <div>
            <b>Browse.</b> Open, navigate and close tabs in their own folder;
            click, type, fill forms and take screenshots.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Read.</b> The page as an accessibility tree with element
            references, its text, the console and the network requests.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Debug.</b> Devtools commands, network mocking and emulation, for
            testing the site you are building on localhost.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Fetch.</b> Send an HTTP request from a tab, as the page would,
            with its origin, CORS rules and cookies. Cookies and authorization
            headers are never returned to the agent.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Run.</b> One JavaScript program that calls the other tools in a
            loop, in a single call, instead of dozens of round trips.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Borrow a profile.</b> Agents start signed in nowhere. When a task
            needs one of your accounts, they can borrow a profile after you
            allow it.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="safety">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <span class="tag">You stay in charge</span>
          <h2>Lilo asks before it matters.</h2>
          <p>
            Agents never take your keyboard, your mouse or your tabs, and the
            important clicks wait for you.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="shields two">
          <div class="shield">
            <span class="sic" style="background:#b39df4">
              <svg
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                width="22"
                height="22"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.8"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <rect x="3" y="4" width="8" height="16" rx="2" />
                <rect x="13" y="4" width="8" height="16" rx="2" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Their own Space</h3>
              <p>
                Agents work in the Agents Space, in a throwaway profile wiped
                after each session. They never see or act on a private Space.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="shield">
            <span class="sic" style="background:#f4bb8c">
              <svg
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                width="22"
                height="22"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.8"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <path d="M12 3 4 6v6c0 4.5 3.4 8.3 8 9 4.6-.7 8-4.5 8-9V6z" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Sends, pays, deletes</h3>
              <p>
                Before an agent clicks something that sends, pays, buys,
                deletes, publishes or signs up, Lilo asks: Allow Once, Always
                Allow on the site, or Deny.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="shield">
            <span class="sic" style="background:#9bd3b4">
              <svg
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                width="22"
                height="22"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.8"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <rect x="5" y="10" width="14" height="10" rx="2" />
                <path d="M8 10V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Approved once, by program</h3>
              <p>
                The first time a client connects, you allow it. The approval is
                tied to the program behind the connection, not to the name it
                announces.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="shield">
            <span class="sic" style="background:#9ac7eb">
              <svg
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                width="22"
                height="22"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.8"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <path d="M6 3h9l4 4v14H6z" />
                <path d="M14 3v5h5" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Your files and cookies</h3>
              <p>
                Lilo always asks before an agent hands a page one of your files,
                and before a request other than GET or HEAD goes out with the
                cookies of one of your profiles.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="connect">
      <div class="wrap steps">
        <div class="head" style="margin:0">
          <span class="tag">Connecting</span>
          <h2>One switch, one click.</h2>
          <p>
            Agents connect to Lilo over a local socket on your Mac.{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/docs/AI-AGENTS.md">
              Read the agent docs
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="claims">
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="ic" style="background:#b39df4">
              1
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Turn it on</h3>
              <p>
                Settings, AI agents, "Let AI agents use Lilo". It is off until
                you do.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="ic" style="background:#9ac7eb">
              2
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Connect your client</h3>
              <p>
                Connect adds Lilo to Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex or
                Cursor. Any other MCP client copies the configuration.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="ic" style="background:#9bd3b4">
              3
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Ask your agent</h3>
              <p>
                Its tabs appear in the Agents Space as it works, and Lilo asks
                you when a click matters.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="faq">
      <div class="wrap faq">
        <div class="head" style="margin:0">
          <span class="tag">Questions</span>
          <h2>About agents.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details>
            <summary>Does Lilo have AI built in?</summary>
            <p>
              No. Lilo ships no model and no assistant. It gives the agents you
              already run a browser to work in, through its MCP server.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Which agents work with Lilo?</summary>
            <p>
              Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex and Cursor connect in one
              click. Any other MCP client works with the configuration Lilo
              copies for you.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can agents see my tabs and accounts?</summary>
            <p>
              Not by default. They work in the Agents Space, in a profile wiped
              after each session and signed in nowhere. They can borrow one of
              your profiles for a task after you allow it, and never see a
              private Space.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is the approval prompt a sandbox?</summary>
            <p>
              No. It is a safety net for well-behaved agents. Code an agent runs
              itself, through JavaScript or devtools commands, is not covered by
              it, so connect only agents you trust.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Does anything go through a server?</summary>
            <p>
              Lilo runs no server and sends nothing to us. Agents talk to it
              over a local socket. What an agent reads goes to the model
              provider that agent uses, as with any other tool it runs.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is it free?</summary>
            <p>
              Yes. Lilo, its MCP server included, is free and open source under
              the{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/LICENSE">
                MIT license
              </a>
              .
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap end">
        <span class="tag">Free and open source</span>
        <Flower id="end-flower" class="mark small-mark" />
        <h2>Give your agent a browser.</h2>
        <p>
          Turn on AI agents in Settings, connect your client, and watch it work
          beside you.
        </p>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center">
          <a
            class="pill dark"
            href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/releases/latest/download/Lilo.dmg"
            style="padding:14px 22px"
          >
            Download for Mac
          </a>
          <a
            class="pill soft"
            href="/"
            style="padding:14px 22px;background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint) 30%, var(--surface))"
          >
            Back to Lilo
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
